## Introduction: The Couch as a Philosophical Problem

The premise of Richard Pearson’s *The Couch* is deceptively simple. Lois, an elderly resident of Eigen, believes that she owns an extraordinarily valuable couch. She has had it appraised at ten million dollars, insured it, and made it the object of considerable social attention. Edward, however, sees an empty living room. There is no couch. Lois is sitting on the floor.

What initially appears to be an exercise in absurdist comedy gradually becomes an examination of a society in which the distinction between reality and fabrication has become unstable. The imaginary couch acquires financial significance, generates commercial activity, influences social relationships, and becomes a source of institutional power. Around it develops a larger system involving surveillance, psychological manipulation, coercion, political complicity, and the suppression of dissent.

The central intellectual question of the novella is not simply why Lois believes in something that does not exist. It is how that belief becomes socially consequential, how other people benefit from it, how institutions accommodate and exploit it, and how the resulting system protects itself against those who challenge it.

The couch is therefore more than a metaphor for delusion. It is a model of manufactured reality. Its physical absence is precisely what makes its social and institutional consequences so revealing.

The novella becomes particularly interesting when considered in relation to six areas of intellectual inquiry. Epistemology concerns how people know what is true; ontology examines what exists and what constitutes reality; political philosophy investigates authority and power; social psychology explains the mechanisms of group belief and participation; propaganda studies examine the organized production of public perception; and narrative theory considers how stories establish, manipulate, and revise accounts of reality.

These disciplines converge in Pearson’s fictional town. The book’s underlying proposition is that reality can be distorted not only through individual error but through the interaction of belief, institutional recognition, economic incentives, social reinforcement, and coercive authority.

## I. Epistemology: The Corruption of Knowledge and the Failure of Recognition

Epistemology is the philosophical study of knowledge, justified belief, evidence, and the conditions under which a proposition can be regarded as true. Its central questions include how knowledge is acquired, what constitutes adequate evidence, and how people distinguish knowledge from unsupported belief (Steup and Neta).

*The Couch* dramatizes these questions through the conflicting perceptions of Lois and Edward.

Edward enters Lois’s house and immediately notices the absence of furniture. Lois, by contrast, describes her couch, gestures toward it, and demonstrates her attachment to it. She even identifies its supposed color, although she cannot settle on whether it is cobalt blue or turquoise (Pearson 5–6). The scene establishes an epistemological conflict: two individuals occupy the same physical environment but understand it in radically different ways.

The important feature is that Lois’s belief is not isolated. She possesses what appears to be corroborating evidence. There is a framed insurance document, an appraisal, a commercial establishment associated with the couch, and a community that discusses it. Her belief is supported by an apparent network of testimony and institutional documentation.

This complicates the conventional distinction between knowledge and belief. Lois does not merely insist that the couch exists despite contrary evidence. She inhabits a social environment in which the supposed evidence for its existence is continually reproduced.

The furniture-store episode makes this problem explicit. The manager reveals that the document is not an actual insurance policy. Its reverse side contains the statement, “This is not an actual insurance policy.” The handwritten valuation Lois interprets as ten million dollars is, according to the manager, actually marked “nil.” Nevertheless, the document has become commercially useful. Other customers request similar policies, and the manager discovers that the arrangement increases sales and allows customers to obtain credit (Pearson 30–34).

Here Pearson introduces a distinction between evidence and the appearance of evidence. The document does not establish the existence of the couch. It establishes that someone has produced a document that can be interpreted as establishing its existence.

This is an important epistemological distinction. A belief may appear well supported because several sources confirm it, but those sources may not be independent or reliable. The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy’s discussion of epistemology emphasizes that justification and truth are distinct problems: a person may possess apparently persuasive grounds for a belief without actually knowing that the belief is true. The example of records and testimony that all derive from a common mistake illustrates precisely this difficulty (Steup and Neta). 

The novella extends that problem into an institutional environment. Its fictional society manufactures corroboration. The furniture store, financial arrangements, group communications, and public behavior reinforce a proposition whose physical basis is absent.

Edward’s discussion with the furniture-store manager introduces another epistemological dimension: the difficulty of recoding contradictory information. The manager describes a condition in which people cannot adequately revise their established understanding of reality. Edward explains that the mind becomes increasingly defensive, expending more effort to preserve its existing reality and reject contradictory information. He characterizes this process as a form of self-imprisonment (Pearson 33–34).

This fictional account has affinities with contemporary discussions of motivated reasoning and belief perseverance. It should not, however, be treated as a clinical description of an established medical condition. The novella’s sociogenic brain disease is a speculative narrative mechanism. Its philosophical significance lies in the question it raises: what happens when the cognitive processes through which people recognize and evaluate evidence become resistant to correction?

The episode involving the county fair develops this problem further. Edward asks Doris and John independently to identify the color of his hat. Their responses differ: John identifies orange, while Doris initially writes yellow and then changes her answer to indicate that Edward is not wearing a hat (Pearson 41–45). The exercise demonstrates that perception itself has become unreliable within Eigen.

The consequences extend beyond Lois. People cannot consistently agree on what they see, and disagreement becomes a potential indication of illness, disloyalty, or doubt.

The epistemological significance is substantial. The problem is no longer that people hold incorrect beliefs. It is that the society has become unable to maintain reliable procedures for distinguishing truth from falsehood.

**Pearson’s central epistemological question is therefore whether truth can remain socially effective when the mechanisms used to recognize, verify, and communicate it have become compromised.**

## II. Ontology: The Social Construction of What Exists

If epistemology asks how people know what is real, ontology asks what it means for something to exist.

The imaginary couch presents an especially productive ontological problem because its physical nonexistence does not prevent it from having consequences.

There is no couch in Lois’s living room. Yet there is an insurance document, a financial arrangement, a commercial enterprise, and an entire network of people whose conduct is influenced by the supposed object.

This distinction invites comparison with John Searle’s *The Construction of Social Reality* (1995). Searle distinguishes between brute facts, which exist independently of human agreement, and institutional facts, which depend on collectively recognized rules and social practices. Money, property, government, and marriage are examples of social realities maintained through collective recognition (Searle).

Searle’s account is particularly relevant to the furniture-store episode. A piece of paper does not become valuable currency through its physical composition alone. It acquires monetary significance because a system of collective recognition assigns it that function. Similarly, property titles and financial instruments derive their institutional authority from shared rules and recognition.

Pearson’s satire pushes this principle into an absurd but revealing territory.

The couch itself does not acquire physical existence. Rather, its supposed existence acquires institutional consequences. The distinction is essential. The document is real. The financial transaction is real. The commercial exploitation is real. The couch is not.

The novella therefore does not simply argue that reality is socially constructed. It investigates the limits of social construction.

Collective agreement can establish the institutional status of an object, but it cannot make a physically absent object physically present. The society of Eigen has confused these two dimensions of existence.

The manager’s explanation is especially telling. He issues policies for imaginary couches, pets, beds, houses, and boats. Customers use the documents to qualify for installment payments, while the manager charges them double for actual furniture. They are more concerned about losing their imaginary possessions than the actual goods they have purchased (Pearson 31–32).

The system has become economically rational within its own fabricated premises.

This is a profound satirical observation. An institution can operate successfully according to rules that are internally consistent while resting on a fundamentally false assumption.

The same problem appears in the larger political structure of Eigen. The town's ownership arrangements, Lois’s family history, and the hidden financial interests described by Edward suggest that institutional reality has been shaped by a historical fiction that has become self-perpetuating (Pearson 91–92).

The novella thus presents a society in which physical reality, social recognition, and institutional authority no longer reliably correspond.

The ontological question becomes: Can something nonexistent become socially real?

Pearson’s answer is more precise than a simple affirmation. The couch never becomes physically real. Its representation becomes socially and institutionally operative. That distinction is the foundation of the satire.

## III. Political Philosophy: The Power to Define and Enforce Reality

The political implications of *The Couch* emerge when the manufacture of falsehood becomes an instrument of institutional control.

The novella’s surveillance operation is not merely an intrusion into Edward’s privacy. It is an attempt to control the conditions under which he understands and experiences his environment.

In *Discipline and Punish*, Michel Foucault examines the development of disciplinary power through surveillance, observation, classification, normalization, and the regulation of individual bodies. He argues that modern power is not confined to spectacular displays of sovereign violence. It also operates through dispersed mechanisms that observe, record, assess, and shape behavior (Foucault).

The relevance to *The Couch* is unmistakable.

Edward discovers that the town has installed approximately fifty cameras throughout his house. His movements, sleeping habits, relationships, purchases, and personal activities have been documented. The surveillance extends into a shared digital archive containing photographs, records, and information about his life (Pearson 20–21).

The operation does not merely observe Edward. It seeks to make him an object of knowledge and control.

Foucault’s analysis is useful because it identifies surveillance as a mechanism through which power produces information about individuals and uses that information to regulate them. In Eigen, the surveillance apparatus is combined with technological manipulation, harassment, and coercion. Edward is not simply watched; his experiences are altered and his ability to establish what is happening is undermined.

The more disturbing dimension emerges through the treatment of people identified as Doubters. The novella describes the use of chips, inserted voices, forced movement, and other forms of coercion. Edward eventually characterizes Eigen as a high-tech prison (Pearson 91).

This is political power operating at the level of individual autonomy.

The question is not merely whether the authorities possess the ability to punish dissent. It is whether they can interfere with the conditions that make independent thought and action possible.

Hannah Arendt’s essay “Truth and Politics” offers another important framework. Arendt argues that factual truth is indispensable to political life because meaningful political debate requires access to a shared factual world. Organized lying threatens this world by undermining the distinction between factual information and deliberate fabrication (Arendt).

This argument illuminates the political significance of the novella’s official accounts.

Doni’s departure from Eigen is described through an official narrative that presents her as having returned home, resigned from her government position, liquidated her assets, moved abroad, and arranged for the removal of her chip. The narrator then declares that this is the only account that exists and the only one that should matter (Pearson 28–29).

The wording is deliberately chilling. The official account is not necessarily presented as the truth. Its authority comes from its status as the only recognized account.

The problem is therefore not simply that institutions lie. It is that institutions may acquire the power to determine which accounts are admissible as reality.

The Sentinel meeting intensifies this political satire. The group includes the President, Vice President, and representatives of banking, technology, and defense. When the participants openly acknowledge that their purpose is world domination, they immediately encounter the contradiction between that purpose and their official bylaws, which refer to global security, stable financial markets, health, human rights, and world peace (Pearson 89–90).

The scene exposes the relationship between public justification and private purpose. Institutional language becomes a mechanism for disguising the actual objectives of power.

Edward is invited to explain the situation in Eigen, but the meeting turns into a demonstration of the group's ability to manipulate and physically control him. His presentation is interrupted by involuntary movements, and the representatives treat his suffering as entertainment (Pearson 91–93).

The political significance is unmistakable: the institutions that might be expected to investigate the abuse are themselves implicated in it.

The narrator’s preview of the subsequent courtroom scene extends this criticism into legal discourse. The government lawyer attempts to redefine slavery so narrowly that Edward’s escape from Eigen supposedly invalidates his claim to have been enslaved. He also objects to the emotional force of the word *slavery* and tries to replace substantive questions of rights with technical distinctions and semantic evasions (Pearson 97–98).

The scene satirizes legal formalism detached from moral substance.

Taken together, these episodes raise a fundamental question of political philosophy:

**What legitimacy can institutions claim when they manufacture the conditions of abuse, control the information about that abuse, and then use their authority to deny that the abuse has occurred?**

## IV. Social Psychology: Conformity, Complicity, and Collective Delusion

The novella’s social psychology is especially sophisticated because it does not assume that everyone in Eigen believes the same things.

Some people sincerely accept the false reality. Others recognize its absurdity but exploit it. Still others participate because they enjoy the spectacle, fear retaliation, or find conformity easier than dissent.

This distinction is central to understanding the social mechanisms of the town.

Leon Festinger’s theory of cognitive dissonance provides one useful framework. In *A Theory of Cognitive Dissonance* (1957), Festinger argues that inconsistencies among beliefs, attitudes, and actions produce psychological discomfort. Individuals are motivated to reduce this discomfort, sometimes by changing their beliefs and sometimes by rationalizing contradictory information or avoiding evidence that would intensify the inconsistency (Festinger).

The theory helps explain Lois’s resistance to Edward.

The couch is not merely a proposition she happens to believe. It is part of her identity, her social position, and her understanding of her environment. Abandoning the belief would require her to confront the implications of an entire life organized around something that never existed.

Her defensive response to Edward is therefore more than disagreement. It is a refusal to reconsider the foundations of her reality.

But Festinger’s theory alone cannot explain Eigen. The novella is equally interested in people who are not psychologically committed to the falsehood.

The furniture-store manager is a particularly important example. He knows the couch does not exist. He understands that the policy is not genuine insurance. Nevertheless, he continues issuing documents because the arrangement increases his sales and profits (Pearson 30–34).

He is not a victim of the same belief system as Lois. He is an enabler.

This introduces a distinction between sincere belief and strategic participation.

The group chat provides another example. Lois believes she commands a large group of supporters, but the narrator explains that many participants are interested primarily in observing Edward’s activities and enjoying the spectacle of his harassment (Pearson 46–47). Their participation does not necessarily depend on accepting Lois’s version of reality.

The distinction matters because collective delusion does not require universal conviction.

A social system can be maintained by people who believe, people who pretend to believe, people who profit, and people who simply refuse to intervene.

The restaurant sequence in “Lunch with the Lieutenant” makes this especially grotesque. Edward is subjected to a coordinated public food assault. Participants invent reasons to punish him, including old grievances and petty personal resentments. One woman approaches under the pretense of sympathy, only to ask him to eat chicken for the amusement of her children (Pearson 86–88).

The violence becomes entertainment.

The episode is not merely an illustration of mob behavior. It depicts the transformation of moral responsibility into collective spectacle. Individual participants can regard their own actions as insignificant because they are part of a larger event.

This resembles processes discussed in social psychology under the headings of conformity, deindividuation, diffusion of responsibility, and moral disengagement.

The novella also develops an important concept through the Doubters. As people begin questioning the existence of missing objects and the reliability of their own perceptions, the town becomes increasingly uncertain about who is a Doubter and who is not. The resulting confusion produces what John calls “Meta-Reality” (Pearson 78–80).

This is an important development. The system's attempt to identify and punish dissent eventually makes the category of dissent itself unstable.

The town no longer knows whom to target.

The social-psychological implication is that collective conformity can become self-undermining. A system that depends on identifying outsiders may eventually produce so much suspicion that it cannot reliably distinguish insiders from outsiders.

The most consequential observation is that **a society does not need everyone to believe a falsehood. It needs enough people to behave as though the falsehood must be protected.**

## V. Propaganda: The Organization of Perception and Public Belief

Propaganda is often understood as the deliberate dissemination of false information. A broader understanding includes the organized shaping of public perception, social attitudes, and the interpretation of events.

Edward Bernays’s *Propaganda* (1928) is a foundational work in the history of modern public relations. Bernays argues that public opinion can be organized through the strategic use of communication, social institutions, and the psychological motivations of audiences. His account is particularly relevant because he treats propaganda as a systematic practice rather than simply a collection of political lies (Bernays).

Pearson’s Eigen is a fictional environment in which these mechanisms operate at an extreme level.

The group chat is a central instrument. It distributes information about Edward, circulates photographs, encourages observation, and creates a coordinated audience for his humiliation. Lois’s supposed supporters become participants in a system that determines what information is circulated and how Edward is interpreted (Pearson 9–13, 20).

The surveillance operation supplies the raw information. The group chat supplies the audience. The harassment supplies the spectacle.

Together, these elements create a mechanism for manufacturing public consensus.

Doni’s encounter with Edward provides an even more direct illustration of propaganda and information control. She knows that Edward is being manipulated, but her communication with him is monitored. She initially prepares to explain the truth in a written note, then produces a much less informative message because she believes the communication is compromised. The surveillance system interferes not only with what Edward knows but with what Doni can safely tell him (Pearson 23–28).

The manipulation of communication is therefore central to the preservation of the system.

The novella also illustrates the importance of official narratives. Doni’s departure is followed by a definitive account that is presented as the only account that matters. The assertion is a concise representation of propaganda’s institutional dimension: control over the accepted record can be more consequential than the ability to persuade every individual that the record is true.

The Sentinel meeting provides the political equivalent. The representatives openly acknowledge their actual ambitions, yet their formal language continues to describe the organization through the vocabulary of security, human rights, and world peace (Pearson 89–90).

The contradiction between declared mission and actual purpose is itself a propaganda mechanism.

The institution does not need to convince its members of the official language. It needs that language to remain available as a public justification.

The novella consequently suggests that propaganda works through several interdependent mechanisms:

- Control of information.
- Repetition and social reinforcement.
- The construction of public enemies.
- The use of spectacle to normalize cruelty.
- The production of official accounts.
- The strategic use of institutional language.
- The suppression or discrediting of competing testimony.

The most important point is that propaganda in *The Couch* does not merely persuade people to believe something false. It helps create an environment in which the distinction between truth and falsehood becomes increasingly difficult to establish.

## VI. Narrative Theory: The Narrator as an Agent of Reality Construction

Perhaps the most distinctive literary feature of *The Couch* is its narrator.

The narrator is not an invisible presence who simply reports events. He comments on the story, addresses the reader, withholds information, anticipates reactions, interrupts scenes, and sometimes revises what has already been presented.

This makes narrative theory essential to understanding the novella.

Wayne C. Booth’s *The Rhetoric of Fiction* (1961; revised edition 1983) established influential concepts for examining the relationship among authors, narrators, implied authors, and readers. Booth’s distinction between reliable and unreliable narration remains particularly useful in analyzing fiction in which the narrator’s account cannot be accepted without qualification (Booth).

The narrator in *The Couch*, however, is more complicated than a conventional unreliable narrator. He frequently reveals that he is manipulating the account. His unreliability is partly performative and self-conscious.

The clearest example occurs in “Lunch with the Lieutenant.”

The narrator first presents a restaurant scene in which Edward appears to be meeting a man who behaves like a childish and evasive version of Lieutenant Taylor. The episode develops into a comic scene involving a poked chicken and a restaurant-wide performance.

Afterward, the narrator directly addresses the reader, acknowledges that the account was not exactly what happened, and announces that the record must be corrected. The narrative then restarts the encounter, revealing that the earlier scene was a deliberately misleading version of events (Pearson 81–85).

This is more than a comic reversal.

The narrator has demonstrated that he can establish an apparently complete version of reality, allow the reader to accept it, and then replace it with another version.

The reader becomes an experimental subject.

The device has a strong relationship to metafiction, particularly Patricia Waugh’s *Metafiction: The Theory and Practice of Self-Conscious Fiction* (1984). Waugh describes metafiction as fiction that draws attention to its own constructed status and exposes the conventions through which narrative creates an apparently coherent world (Waugh).

Pearson’s narrator repeatedly makes the reader conscious of that construction.

At one point, the narrator explains that Edward cannot hear him because he is “words on a page” (Pearson 19). Elsewhere, he withholds information because revealing it would spoil the plot. He interrupts the narrative to instruct the reader to take a break. He later admits that an account was inaccurate and must be corrected.

These interventions make the storytelling process part of the novella’s intellectual subject.

The connection to the other philosophical dimensions is important.

Within Eigen, institutions manipulate the information through which individuals understand their environment. The narrator performs a literary equivalent: he controls the information through which the reader understands the fictional environment.

The narrator is not identical to the political institutions in the story, and his manipulation is frequently comic rather than coercive. Nevertheless, the formal parallel is significant.

Both the narrator and the institutions exercise authority through control of information.

The *Narrator’s Note* makes this relationship explicit. The narrator announces his retirement, refuses to explain the origin of the couch, comments on anticipated criticism of structural issues and tonal inconsistency, and directly addresses an AI critic. He even instructs the critic to award the book five stars (Pearson 97).

The note is an unusually direct act of metafictional self-consciousness. The narrator acknowledges the existence of literary criticism and attempts to influence the terms on which the work will be judged.

This is not simply an authorial joke. It extends the novella’s concern with authority over interpretation into the relationship between the text and its reader.

The final refusal to explain the couch’s origin is particularly revealing. The narrator says that he could explain how it came to be, but will not, adding that he has already told the reader.

The reader is left with an unresolved question about the relationship between narrative disclosure and narrative authority.

The narrator has given us enough information to recognize the central absurdity, but he retains the power to withhold its ultimate explanation.

In this respect, the form of the novella participates in its philosophical argument.

**The reader, like the characters, must confront the problem of how much to trust an account of reality when the person controlling that account has the power to manipulate it.**

## VII. The Convergence of the Six Intellectual Traditions

The six areas of inquiry should not be treated as separate interpretations. Their importance lies in the way they connect.

Epistemology asks how people recognize truth. Ontology asks how social and institutional systems assign reality to things. Social psychology examines why individuals accept or participate in collective beliefs. Propaganda studies explain how those beliefs are reproduced and circulated. Political philosophy examines how institutions enforce the resulting order. Narrative theory investigates how the account of reality itself can be constructed, manipulated, and revised.

In *The Couch*, these mechanisms form an interconnected system.

The following sequence illustrates their relationship:

1. **Epistemology:** People lose the ability or willingness to distinguish accurate perception from established belief.
2. **Ontology:** Collective recognition gives institutional consequences to things that do not physically exist.
3. **Social psychology:** Belief, conformity, self-interest, fear, and entertainment sustain the fabricated reality.
4. **Propaganda:** Information systems, public spectacle, and official accounts reproduce the accepted version of events.
5. **Political philosophy:** Institutions use surveillance, coercion, and authority to protect the system against dissent.
6. **Narrative theory:** The narrator makes the reader confront the construction and authority of accounts of reality.

These processes also reinforce one another. Institutional power shapes public information; public information influences belief; belief sustains institutional arrangements; and those arrangements provide economic and political incentives for continued participation.

The result is a self-reinforcing system.

This helps explain why the novella's episodic structure should not automatically be understood through the expectations of a conventional mystery or thriller. Its chapters reveal different dimensions of a social and political environment. The furniture store, the county fair, the group chat, the missingness phenomenon, the food fight, and Sentinel are not merely separate comic episodes. They expose different mechanisms through which the same constructed reality operates.

The recurring couch provides conceptual continuity, while the expanding cast of institutions and characters reveals the larger system.

## VIII. The Ending: Recognition Is Not the Same as Transformation

The final return to Lois brings the novella's central problem back to its simplest physical expression.

Edward visits Lois after hearing that she has been hospitalized. She continues to describe her living room as furnished and insists that she has been misunderstood. Edward again tells her that there is no couch. He explains that she has been sitting on the floor in an empty room.

After Edward leaves, Lois finally sees the floor. She recognizes the absence of the furniture and screams as she realizes what has happened (Pearson 94–96).

The scene is important because it distinguishes recognition from transformation.

Lois finally recognizes the physical reality that Edward has been describing. But her realization does not automatically undo the institutional arrangements, financial exploitation, political corruption, surveillance, or coercion that have characterized Eigen.

One individual's recognition does not dismantle a social system.

The ending therefore avoids reducing the novella's problem to a simple moral lesson about the importance of seeing what is in front of us. The issue is much larger. Even when truth becomes visible, the structures that have been built around falsehood may remain.

This distinction is central to the political and philosophical implications of the work.

The couch disappears from Lois's understanding, but the social consequences of the couch have already occurred.

The narrator's refusal to explain the couch's origin further complicates the ending. The reader receives a resolution of the immediate perceptual problem but not a complete explanation of the larger phenomenon.

The final note preserves the narrator's authority and the novella's refusal to provide a single, exhaustive account.

## Conclusion: The Reality of the Nonexistent

Richard Pearson’s *The Couch* is most productively understood as an interdisciplinary work of philosophical and political satire. Its central conceit provides a framework for examining the relationship between individual perception, collective belief, institutional recognition, economic exploitation, and political authority.

Its engagement with epistemology reveals the fragility of knowledge when evidence and testimony are compromised. Its ontological implications expose the distinction between physical existence and institutional reality. Its political satire examines the power of institutions to define acceptable truth and suppress dissent. Its social psychology reveals that collective delusion is sustained not only by believers but also by opportunists, conformists, spectators, and enablers. Its treatment of propaganda shows how information control and official narratives manufacture public consensus. Finally, its metafictional narrator makes the reader confront the constructed nature of storytelling itself.

The central insight is that a society does not need to make a falsehood physically true to make it consequentially real.

The couch does not exist, but its supposed existence produces financial transactions, social relationships, institutional arrangements, and political consequences. Some people believe in it. Others exploit it. Still others participate because they find it useful, entertaining, or dangerous to challenge.

That is the deeper significance of the novella's premise.

The book is not simply asking why people believe in an imaginary couch. It is asking how a society becomes organized around something that does not exist, why people continue to participate when they have reason to know better, and what happens to truth when the institutions that should protect it become invested in its suppression.

Its final philosophical distinction is perhaps its most important: **recognizing reality and changing reality are not the same accomplishment.**

Lois finally sees the floor. The reader, however, has been shown an entire society built around the refusal to see it.

